    "title": "At Wake Forest, Duncan Will Show His Staying Power",
    "text": "AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENTBy Curry KirkpatrickNov. 24, 1996See the article in its original context from November 24, 1996, Section 8, Page 7Buy ReprintsNew York Times subscribers* enjoy full access to TimesMachine—view over 150 years of New York Times journalism, as it originally appeared.*Does not include Games-only or Cooking-only subscribers.About the ArchiveThis is a digitized version of an article from The Times’s print archive, before the start of online publication in 1996. To preserve these articles as they originally appeared, The Times does not alter, edit or update them.Occasionally the digitization process introduces transcription errors or other problems; we are continuing to work to improve these archived versions.As it turns out, Tim Duncan was always calm, cool, relaxed; in the marvelous patois of his native Virgin Islands, always ''on the lime'' or ''limin'.'' When Hurricane Hugo ripped through his homeland of St. Croix in 1989, destroying the pool where he was training to become a world-class swimmer, he merely switched sports. When few college coaches from the United States mainland traveled to watch him play pickup basketball on the grassy-knolled outdoor court near Hay Penny Beach, Christiansted, he simply agreed to play for one who did, the low-profile Dave Odom of little old Wake Forest.When coaches speak of attitude, they invariably mean a bad one. But Duncan's is blithe, lilting, memorialized by sunny Doris Day herself: Que sera, sera. Duncan has become unmistakably the most gifted college player in the land. And as his underclassmen peers kept scurrying off to the extremely greener pastures of the National Basketball Association -- ''I wondered if there'd be anybody left to play against,'' he said, laughing -- he continued to be laid back and limin'. That could mean cracking another psychology textbook. Or mastering another of his beloved Sega games. Or hanging out at the mall, strolling the campus, checking out the girls, taking off his shirt, sticking his cap on backward and watching a Wake baseball game. Doing what most regular college guys do. Duncan never hurries, hassles, suffers haranging. He neither gives nor takes heat of any sort. He plays his game the way he lives. ''Timmy doesn't trash talk,'' said his brother-in-law, Ricky Lowery. ''When guys look at the scoreboard, that's when they know they've been trashed.'' Subscribe to The Times to read as many articles as you like.A version of this article appears in print on Nov. 24, 1996, Section 8, Page 7 of the National edition with the headline: At Wake Forest, Duncan Will Show His Staying Power. Order Reprints | Today’s Paper | SubscribeAdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
